    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d330">

                <pb facs="0386" n="1220" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg1220"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 658.119/312</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Page</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-04-16T15:00:00-04:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 16, 1918, 3 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>7301. For Sheldon [from
                    War Trade Board]:</p>
                <p>347. Embassy’s 9439, April 10, 7 p.m., your 404. Swedish <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >modus vivendi</hi>. Glad to learn list of 100,000 tons is practically
                    complete allowing: the licensing of additional rations for Sweden.</p>
                <pb facs="0387" n="1221" xml:id="pg1221"/>
                <list>
                    <label>3.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d330-f1"/>
                    </label>
                    <item>Please give us names of the three ships which sailed from Sweden and
                        stopped at Bergen.</item>
                    <label>4.</label>
                    <item>We are considering matter of phosphate rock and will let the 10,000 tons
                        clear if satisfactory assurances are given of chartering similar amount of
                        tonnage for the period of four months as agreed.</item>
                    <label>6.</label>
                    <item>When will steamship <hi rendition="#italic">Alida</hi> sail from other
                        side? This in order to arrange about fuel oil for her.</item>
                    <label>7.</label>
                    <item>Your understanding is correct. Fifty per cent division will apply to
                        vessels chartered by United States and England under clause 3, same as
                        clause 2. It should be borne in mind that England owes us large amount of
                        tonnage still under Norwegian 50-50 agreement and that this will be subject
                        of discussion between Fisher and Munson Monday. Result of this will be cabled to you.</item>
                </list>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
